    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "L98.9 feels a bit too broad for what we're seeing. The clinicians are describing these lesions as having a very \"gnarled\" or \"rugged\" texture, almost like they're organic growths that follow no standard pattern. If the morphology is highly irregular and doesn't fit the standard pigmented lesion codes in the L-series, how should we handle the specificity for the texture itself? Is there a way to code for the irregular surface or the specific luster/consistency that isn't just dumping it into an 'unspecified' category?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the terms \"gnarled\" or \"rugged\" and other descriptors are consistently used by the clinicians, it might indicate a new spectrum of lesions which are not yet categorized in the standard ICD-10 codes. In such a case, you should first document these terms in your claim files and communicate with your coding department's standing orders or coding guidelines to see if there's flexibility in integrating custom descriptors into the existing coding standards. If updates or new coding options were to be added for such lesions in the future, they would likely fall under the category of 'other disorders of the skin and subcutaneous tissue' (T14.—) once defined and standardized. In the interim, you'll need to enforce precise coding by detailing these descriptors explicitly, and if accepted by the payers, include them as vital signs in the medical documentation to provide a more detailed account of the dermatological issue at hand. Additionally, liaise with the regional medical billing experts or seek a ruling from the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) or relevant payers if there's an ongoing debate on how these codes should be assigned. This particular niche might also require an appeal or a query to the National Correct Coding Initiative (NCCI) editor for guidance, given its unprecedented nature",
      "source": "target",
